Repository grouping various projects and code snippets I contributed to the KM3NeT codebase. Collated at the end of my contract with the collaboration, showing only those for which permission was granted.

Full projects:
- IRF: C++/ROOT repository to read ROOT TTree structures containing simulated and reconstructed KM3NeT events, and turn them into sparse-histogram-like TTrees. This format allows for compact and efficient analysis of KM3NeT sensitivity to neutrino oscillations without the need for dedicated libraries (beyond ROOD) to read the inputs. User can manually input a string to define the path of the information inside the input file and any required transformation of the data. This allows the flexibility to read data from different detector configurations or data formats, including that of other experiments (e.g. IceCube). CI/CD builds docker and singularity images and uploads them to the KM3NeT image registry. Targeted at intermediate KM3NeT members.
- OscillationsWorkflow: Snakemake/Python/Bash workflow pipeline for the generation of analysis results. Combines an IRF image with a KOFI image (separate KM3NeT code repository) to automatically find constraints on neutrino mixing parameters, and can also be used to optimize analysis parameters (e.g. selection cuts on noise vs signal) for improved results. Targeted at beginner KM3NeT members, and written in collaboration with Ivan Mozun Mateo.

Contributions:
- JPP: Arguably the central KM3NeT library, based on C++. Showing here only the snippets I contributed for fast estimation of the effect of quantity and quality of neutrino source candidates in on the potential and reliability of a discovery. Targeted at beginner KM3NeT members (user documentation kept private).
